</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is one of the most basic observations in graph theory that no simple, finite graph <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with at least <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> vertices can be completely irregular, i.e. its vertices cannot have all pairwise distinct degrees. Some alternative concepts of irregular graphs were thus investigated already by Chartrand, Erdős and Oellermann <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, and others, see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.
More recently, a notion of a <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">locally irregular graph</em> was introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.
It is a graph <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> whose adjacent vertices have distinct degrees, i.e. <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G}(u)\neq d_{G}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G}(u)\neq d_{G}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> for every edge <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv\in E</annotation></semantics></math>. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> also a problem of edge decomposability of graphs to locally irregular subgraphs was raised. We say that a graph <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">decomposable</em> to <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> locally irregular subgraphs if its edge set can be partitioned to <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> subsets: <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=E_{1}\cup E_{2}\cup\ldots\cup E_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=E_{1}\cup E_{2}\cup\ldots\cup E_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the subgraph <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}=(V,E_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}=(V,E_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is locally irregular for <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This concept is related with a recent direction of research of Alon and Wei <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, also developed by Fox, Luo and Pham <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, who investigated so-called <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">irregular subgraphs</em>.
See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> for discussion and results linking the two concepts.
The notion of locally irregular graphs is also strongly related with yet another well-known descendant of research on irregular graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> and so-called irregularity strength of graphs, see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, which is the <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">1–2–3 Conjecture</em>. This was posed in 2004 by
Karoński, Łuczak and Thomason <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> and recently confirmed by Keusch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.
It may be phrased as follows. Given any connected graph with at least <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> vertices, one may multiply every its edge <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> to at most <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> copies of <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> so that the resulting multigraph is locally irregular, i.e. its adjacent vertices have pairwise distinct degrees.
See e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> for a more comprehensive discussion on relations between these concepts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Also in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> a family <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{T}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔗</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{T}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of all connected graphs which are not decomposable to any number of locally irregular subgraphs was identified. The central initial problem in the study devoted to locally irregular graphs was the following conjecture.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>)<span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Each connected graph <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> which does not belong to <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{T}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔗</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{T}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is decomposable to <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> locally irregular subgraphs.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">It is worth mentioning that the family <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{T}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔗</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{T}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> includes only graphs with maximum degree upper bounded by <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>. It thus was natural to investigate wether graphs with sufficiently large degrees are decomposable to <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> locally irregular subgraphs.
In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> this was in particular proven to hold for regular graphs, which are in some sense as far from being locally irregular as possible.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>)<span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Each <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graph with <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 10^{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>7</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 10^{7}</annotation></semantics></math> is decomposable to <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> locally irregular subgraphs.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This result was further extended towards all graphs <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with minimum degree <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(G)\geq 10^{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(G)\geq 10^{10}</annotation></semantics></math> in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>.
Exploiting in particular this theorem, Bensmail, Merker and Thomassen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>
proved next that each connected graph outside <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{T}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔗</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{T}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is decomposable to at most <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="328" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>328</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">328</annotation></semantics></math> locally irregular subgraphs. The bound of <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="328" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>328</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">328</annotation></semantics></math> was also latter refined to <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="220" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>220</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">220</annotation></semantics></math> in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>
for other related results concerning locally irregular graphs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Surprisingly, quite recently a single connected exception to Conjecture <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Conjecture 1 ([]). ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> was found
by Sedlar and Škrekovski <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. This is decomposable to <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> locally irregular subgraphs. Consequently, they posed the following less restrictive variant of the conjecture.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>)<span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Each connected graph <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> which does not belong to <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{T}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔗</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{T}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is decomposable to <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> locally irregular subgraphs.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">As the main contribution of this paper we provide the following significant reduction of the upper bound <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>7</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{7}</annotation></semantics></math> from Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 ([]). ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> in context of the new Conjecture <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Conjecture 3 ([]). ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. In a way this concerns the most challenging in face of our goals family of graphs, which are extremely far from being (locally) irregular.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Each <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graph with <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 54000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>54000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 54000</annotation></semantics></math> is decomposable to <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> locally irregular subgraphs.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Let us remark that our approach is significantly different from the one used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> to prove Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 ([]). ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. It benefits in particular, but not exclusively, from the allowed one more locally irregular subgraph, which admitted an entirely new design of a desired decomposition.
Its first phase exploits the probabilistic method in order to initially decompose a given regular graph to <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> subgraphs with relatively small chromatic numbers, where we put some effort to optimize our approach with regard to minimizing the lower bound for <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. In the second, deterministic part of our argument we also make use of results stemming from the mentioned related problems, i.e. certain theorems on degree constrained subgraphs of Addario-Berry, Dalal, McDiarmid, Reed, and Thomason <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, and of Alon and Wei <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
We believe the applied strategy is quite interesting by itself.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">In the second section we shall outline the main ideas of the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Next we shall present several useful tools. Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> contains the complete proof of our main result. The last section includes concluding remarks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Outline of the main ideas</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The main vague idea of the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> is the following.
In the beginning we shall partition the edges of a given <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graph <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> to two subsets. Each of these shall induce a graph <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=0,1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=0,1</annotation></semantics></math>, with several identical features.
First of all each <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> shall be close to a <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graph, but shall have relatively small chromatic number. For this purpose we shall randomly associate to every vertex <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> a pair of integers <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(O_{v},I_{v})\in[K]^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(O_{v},I_{v})\in[K]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, called its colour, where <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is small compared to <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[K]=\{1,2,\ldots,K\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[K]=\{1,2,\ldots,K\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Than we shall colour the edges accordingly with <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, following several rules – the edges coloured <math id="S2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> shall induce <math id="S2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and the ones coloured <math id="S2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> shall form <math id="S2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The values <math id="S2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> shall in fact associate the vertices with independent sets in <math id="S2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (witnessing its relatively small chromatic number) and the values <math id="S2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> shall play the same role for <math id="S2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. In order to maintain a better error control, the aforementioned <math id="S2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-edge partition shall be partly performed deterministically, using Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma 9. ‣ 3. Tools ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> of Alon and Wei.
More specifically, the random vertex colours assignment shall be mainly used to provide large independent sets in <math id="S2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, while the rest of the required features of <math id="S2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> shall be assured due to Lemma <math id="S2.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math>, providing almost perfectly equal distribution of colours <math id="S2.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> in certain distinguished subsets of edges.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Then each of the graphs <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=0,1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=0,1</annotation></semantics></math>, shall be further decomposed into two subgraphs, say <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. These, unlike <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, shall have different sets of properties and features,
which in varied ways shall imply that <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are locally irregular.
The degrees in <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> shall be determined by <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>. More precisely, these shall be (almost) equal to twice one of these values modulo <math id="S2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2K</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus shall be different for adjacent vertices from different independent sets mentioned above. In order to construct such <math id="S2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> we shall use Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Lemma 8. ‣ 3. Tools ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, concerning existence in every graph very restrictively degree constrained subgraphs, which essentially was
developed by Addario-Berry, Dalal, McDiarmid, Reed and Thomason while conducting research over the 1–2–3 Conjecture. In order to assure that the complement of <math id="S2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is <math id="S2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, is locally irregular as well, we shall on the other hand first identify potentially risky edges from the point of view of <math id="S2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
All of these shall be included in <math id="S2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> prior to using Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Lemma 8. ‣ 3. Tools ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, which shall allow us to avoid degree conflicts in <math id="S2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As the values <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(O_{v},I_{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(O_{v},I_{v})</annotation></semantics></math> shall be chosen randomly and independently for every <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math>, some pairs of adjacent vertices shall receive identical colours. If this occurs we shall uncolour both of them. We shall however show that the set <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> of such uncolored vertices is relatively sparse, and thus we shall be able to handle them separately, using a natural greedy approach. In fact these vertices shall be dealt with before the remaining ones.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Tools</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We shall use several tools useful while applying the probabilistic method.
In particular some concentration inequalities:
the well-known Chernoff Bound and the McDiarmid’s Inequality, cf. e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<span id="Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Chernoff Bound</span>)<span id="Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1},X_{2},\ldots,X_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1},X_{2},\ldots,X_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be independent random variables taking values in <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\sum_{i=1}^{n}X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\sum_{i=1}^{n}X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=\mathbb{E}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=\mathbb{E}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for all <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}\left(X\geq(1+\delta)\mu\right)\leq\left(\frac{e^{\delta}}{(1+\delta)^{1+\delta}}\right)^{\mu}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>δ</mi></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}\left(X\geq(1+\delta)\mu\right)\leq\left(\frac{e^{\delta}}{(1+\delta)^{1+\delta}}\right)^{\mu}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note the same upper bound holds if we only know that <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\geq\mathbb{E}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\geq\mathbb{E}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<span id="Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">McDiarmid’s Inequality</span>)<span id="Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=Z(W_{1},W_{2},\ldots,W_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=Z(W_{1},W_{2},\ldots,W_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> be a random variable that depends on <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> independent random variables <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1},W_{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1},W_{2},</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ldots," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ldots,</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose
that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|Z(W_{1},\ldots,W_{i},\ldots,W_{n})-Z(W_{1},\ldots,W^{\prime}_{i},\ldots,W_{n})|\leq c_{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|Z(W_{1},\ldots,W_{i},\ldots,W_{n})-Z(W_{1},\ldots,W^{\prime}_{i},\ldots,W_{n})|\leq c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for all <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1},W_{2},\ldots,W_{n},W^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1},W_{2},\ldots,W_{n},W^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for all <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}\left(Z\geq\mathbb{E}(Z)+t\right)\leq e^{-\frac{t^{2}}{2\sum_{i=1}^{n}c_{i}^{2}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo maxsize="0.500em" minsize="0.500em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mstyle><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}\left(Z\geq\mathbb{E}(Z)+t\right)\leq e^{-\frac{t^{2}}{2\sum_{i=1}^{n}c_{i}^{2}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We shall also need a variant of the symmetric Lovász Local Lemma, involving conditional probabilities of bad events, cf. e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> (Corollary 5.1.2 and comments beneath its proof).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<span id="Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lovász Local Lemma</span>)<span id="Thmtheorem7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}=\{A_{1},A_{2},\ldots,A_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}=\{A_{1},A_{2},\ldots,A_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be a family of
events in an arbitrary probability space.
Suppose that for every <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math> there is a set <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(A_{i})\subset\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(A_{i})\subset\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> of size at most <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> such that for each <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}\subseteq\mathcal{A}\smallsetminus(\Gamma(A_{i})\cup\{A_{i}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}\subseteq\mathcal{A}\smallsetminus(\Gamma(A_{i})\cup\{A_{i}\})</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}\left(A_{i}|\bigcap_{B\in\mathcal{B}}\overline{B}\right)\leq\frac{1}{e(D+1)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋂</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></munder><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}\left(A_{i}|\bigcap_{B\in\mathcal{B}}\overline{B}\right)\leq\frac{1}{e(D+1)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem7.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}\left(\bigcap_{i=1}^{n}\overline{A_{i}}\right)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⋂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}\left(\bigcap_{i=1}^{n}\overline{A_{i}}\right)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For the deterministic part of our proof we shall also need the following lemma from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> (Corollary 9), concerning degree constrained subgraph of a given graph, which is a direct consequence of Theorem 2.2 due to Addario-Berry, Dalal, McDiarmid, Reed and Thomason from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>
(see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> for similar degree theorems and their applications).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 8</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that for some graph <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> with minimum degree at least <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> we have chosen, for every
vertex <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, an integer <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{v}\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{v}\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6\lambda_{v}\leq d(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6\lambda_{v}\leq d(v)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for every assignment</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t:V\to\mathbb{Z}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t:V\to\mathbb{Z},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">there exists a spanning subgraph <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{H}(v)\in[\frac{d(v)}{3},\frac{2d(v)}{3}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{H}(v)\in[\frac{d(v)}{3},\frac{2d(v)}{3}]</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{H}(v)\equiv t(v)\pmod{\lambda_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{H}(v)\equiv t(v)\pmod{\lambda_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{H}(v)\equiv t(v)+1\pmod{\lambda_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{H}(v)\equiv t(v)+1\pmod{\lambda_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We shall also use the following lemma due to Alon and Wei <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> (see Lemma 4.1).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 9</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph, and let <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z:E\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z:E\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> be a weight function assigning to each edge <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in E</annotation></semantics></math> a real weight <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z(e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z(e)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is a function <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x:E\to\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x:E\to\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> assigning to each edge an integer value in <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> so that for every <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{e\ni v}z(e)-1&lt;\sum_{e\ni v}x(e)\leq\sum_{e\ni v}z(e)+1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∋</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">&lt;</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∋</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∋</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{e\ni v}z(e)-1&lt;\sum_{e\ni v}x(e)\leq\sum_{e\ni v}z(e)+1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph and let <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v,w\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v,w\in V</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}\subseteq V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}\subseteq V</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}\subseteq E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊆</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}\subseteq E</annotation></semantics></math>.
By <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{G}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{G}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> we understand the set of neighbours of <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
We denote by <math id="S3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> the set of edges in <math id="S3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> incident with <math id="S3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.
We also define <math id="S3.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{E^{\prime}}(v)=|E^{\prime}(v)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{E^{\prime}}(v)=|E^{\prime}(v)|</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{V^{\prime}}(v)=|N_{G}(v)\cap V^{\prime}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{V^{\prime}}(v)=|N_{G}(v)\cap V^{\prime}|</annotation></semantics></math>.
Finally, by <math id="S3.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm dist}_{G}(v,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>dist</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm dist}_{G}(v,w)</annotation></semantics></math> we denote the distance between <math id="S3.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a></h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Random colour assignment and distinguished sets</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graph with <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 54000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>54000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 54000</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let us fix a few optimized constants:</p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0.025,\hskip 14.22636pts=0.0031,\hskip 14.22636ptr=0.26,\hskip 14.22636ptu=0.131." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.025</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.587em">,</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0031</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.587em">,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.26</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.587em">,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.131</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0.025,\hskip 14.22636pts=0.0031,\hskip 14.22636ptr=0.26,\hskip 14.22636ptu=0.131.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Set</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\left\lceil kd\right\rceil." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\left\lceil kd\right\rceil.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Let us randomly assign to every vertex <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> a colour <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{v}=(O_{v},I_{v})\in[K]^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{v}=(O_{v},I_{v})\in[K]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{v},I_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{v},I_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math>, are independent random variables uniformly distributed over <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[K]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[K]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denote the set of vertices to be uncoloured by</p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="U=\{v\in V~|~\exists w\in N_{G}(v):C_{w}=C_{v}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.330em" rspace="0.497em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=\{v\in V~|~\exists w\in N_{G}(v):C_{w}=C_{v}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">and let <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>e</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{e}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of edges induced by <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="U^{e}=\{vw\in E:v,w\in U\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>e</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{e}=\{vw\in E:v,w\in U\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">We further distinguish the set of edges ,,<em id="S4.SS1.p1.5.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">touching</em>” <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\{vw\in E:v\in U\vee w\in U\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\{vw\in E:v\in U\vee w\in U\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.6" class="ltx_p">and the set of <em id="S4.SS1.p1.6.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">special</em> edges:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\{vw\in E\smallsetminus T:O_{v}=O_{w}\vee I_{v}=I_{w}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\{vw\in E\smallsetminus T:O_{v}=O_{w}\vee I_{v}=I_{w}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.7" class="ltx_p">We also define a certain set of <em id="S4.SS1.p1.7.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">risky edges</em>, where by <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|m-n|_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|m-n|_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> we understand the <em id="S4.SS1.p1.7.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">distance</em> between integers <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S4.SS1.p1.7.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">modulo</em> <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|m-n|_{K}=\min\{m-n~({\rm mod}~K),~n-m~({\rm mod}~K)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.330em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.330em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|m-n|_{K}=\min\{m-n~({\rm mod}~K),~n-m~({\rm mod}~K)\}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\left\{vw\in E\smallsetminus T:1\leq|O_{v}-O_{w}|_{K}\leq\frac{sd+7}{2}\vee 1\leq|I_{v}-I_{w}|_{K}\leq\frac{sd+7}{2}\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>∨</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\left\{vw\in E\smallsetminus T:1\leq|O_{v}-O_{w}|_{K}\leq\frac{sd+7}{2}\vee 1\leq|I_{v}-I_{w}|_{K}\leq\frac{sd+7}{2}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.8" class="ltx_p">and the set of edges which are risky but not special:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\prime}=R\smallsetminus S." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\prime}=R\smallsetminus S.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.9" class="ltx_p">Finally, we define two more specific subsets of edges:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}=E\smallsetminus(T\cup R),\hskip 28.45274ptE^{\prime\prime}=E\smallsetminus(T\cup R\cup S)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="3.017em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}=E\smallsetminus(T\cup R),\hskip 28.45274ptE^{\prime\prime}=E\smallsetminus(T\cup R\cup S).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We shall first prove that we may choose such a vertex colours assignment so that each vertex <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is adjacent with bounded numbers of vertices of certain types. For clarity it shall usually be more convenient to refer further on to the corresponding edges, joining a given vertex <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> with vertices of some types. The only exception adheres to <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{U}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{U}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>, which shall always mean the number of neighbours <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in U</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="vw\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">vw\in E</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. shall always refer to the neighbourhood of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> in the entire <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Assuring desired features of the distinguished sets</h3>

<div id="Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_claim">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claim 10</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">With positive probability, for every <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{S}(v)&lt;sd" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{S}(v)&lt;sd</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{R}(v)&lt;rd" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{R}(v)&lt;rd</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{U}(v)&lt;ud" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{U}(v)&lt;ud</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">For every <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math>, let us denote the events:</span></p>
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{v}:~d_{S}(v)\geq sd" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.608em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{v}:~d_{S}(v)\geq sd</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{v}:~d_{R}(v)\geq rd" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.608em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{v}:~d_{R}(v)\geq rd</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{v}:~d_{U}(v)\geq ud" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.608em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{v}:~d_{U}(v)\geq ud</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of all such events.
For every <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{v}\in\{S_{v},R_{v},U_{v}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{v}\in\{S_{v},R_{v},U_{v}\}</annotation></semantics></math> we define:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Gamma(A_{v})=\bigcup_{w:~{\rm dist}_{G}(v,w)\leq 3}\{S_{w},R_{w},U_{w}\}\smallsetminus\{A_{v}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.548em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>dist</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(A_{v})=\bigcup_{w:~{\rm dist}_{G}(v,w)\leq 3}\{S_{w},R_{w},U_{w}\}\smallsetminus\{A_{v}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text">Note that for every <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{v}\in\{S_{v},R_{v},U_{v}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{v}\in\{S_{v},R_{v},U_{v}\}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Gamma(A_{v})|\leq 3(d^{3}-d^{2}+d)+2&lt;3d^{3}-1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Gamma(A_{v})|\leq 3(d^{3}-d^{2}+d)+2&lt;3d^{3}-1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text">and</span></p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{v}~{\rm is~determined~by~the~colours}~C_{w}~{\rm of}~w\in V~{\rm such~that~dist}_{G}(v,w)\leq 2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>is</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>determined</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>by</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>the</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>colours</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>of</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>such</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>that</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>dist</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{v}~{\rm is~determined~by~the~colours}~C_{w}~{\rm of}~w\in V~{\rm such~that~dist}_{G}(v,w)\leq 2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Instead of considering <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> itself, we shall focus on its superset, including also edges touching <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{*}=\{vw\in E:O_{v}=O_{w}\vee I_{v}=I_{w}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}=\{vw\in E:O_{v}=O_{w}\vee I_{v}=I_{w}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">We obviously have that <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{S}(v)\leq d_{S^{*}}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{S}(v)\leq d_{S^{*}}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> for each vertex <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider any <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> and any subset <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}\subseteq\mathcal{A}\smallsetminus\left(\Gamma(A_{v})\cup\{A_{v}\}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}\subseteq\mathcal{A}\smallsetminus\left(\Gamma(A_{v})\cup\{A_{v}\}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{v}\in\{S_{v},R_{v},U_{v}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{v}\in\{S_{v},R_{v},U_{v}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. As the event: <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{S^{*}}(v)\geq sd" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{S^{*}}(v)\geq sd</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by the choices of <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> for vertices <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> at distance at most <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, by (<a href="#S4.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 4.2. Assuring desired features of the distinguished sets ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.E4" title="In Proof. ‣ 4.2. Assuring desired features of the distinguished sets ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>), this event is mutually independent of <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(d_{S^{*}}(v)\geq sd~|~\bigcap_{B\in\mathcal{B}}\overline{B})=\mathbb{P}(d_{S^{*}}(v)\geq sd)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0.330em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋂</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(d_{S^{*}}(v)\geq sd~|~\bigcap_{B\in\mathcal{B}}\overline{B})=\mathbb{P}(d_{S^{*}}(v)\geq sd)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Below we thus bound the latter one.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Note that if we fix a colour <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> then each edge <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="vw\in E(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">vw\in E(v)</annotation></semantics></math> is independently included in <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{*}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>
with probability</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\cdot\frac{1}{K}-\frac{1}{K^{2}}&lt;\frac{2}{kd}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\cdot\frac{1}{K}-\frac{1}{K^{2}}&lt;\frac{2}{kd}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">Thus, for any fixed <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\in[K]^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\in[K]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left(d_{S^{*}}(v)~|~C_{v}=C\right)&lt;\frac{2}{k}=80&lt;0.0015d=\mu_{s}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.052em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.330em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>80</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>0.0015</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left(d_{S^{*}}(v)~|~C_{v}=C\right)&lt;\frac{2}{k}=80&lt;0.0015d=\mu_{s}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">(for <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 54000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>54000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 54000</annotation></semantics></math>).
Set <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}=0.0015" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0015</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}=0.0015</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{2}=s-s_{1}=0.0016" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0016</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{2}=s-s_{1}=0.0016</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{s}=\frac{s_{2}}{s_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{s}=\frac{s_{2}}{s_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="sd=(1+\delta_{s})\mu_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">sd=(1+\delta_{s})\mu_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and by the Chernoff Bound, for any <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\in[K]^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\in[K]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\left(d_{S^{*}}(v)\geq sd~|~C_{v}=C\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0.330em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\left(d_{S^{*}}(v)\geq sd~|~C_{v}=C\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\left(d_{S^{*}}(v)\geq(1+\delta_{s})\mu_{s}~|~C_{v}=C\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.330em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\left(d_{S^{*}}(v)\geq(1+\delta_{s})\mu_{s}~|~C_{v}=C\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
