<p id="S4.SS2.p5.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.5.1" class="ltx_text">Again, the last inequality above can be proven via setting <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{3}=e^{r_{2}}/(\frac{r}{r_{1}})^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mi>r</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{3}=e^{r_{2}}/(\frac{r}{r_{1}})^{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{r}(d)=d^{3}(r_{3})^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{r}(d)=d^{3}(r_{3})^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and noting that <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{r}(54000)&lt;0.1&lt;1/(3e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>54000</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{r}(54000)&lt;0.1&lt;1/(3e)</annotation></semantics></math> while <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is decreasing for <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> at least <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3/\ln(r_{3}^{-1})&lt;4592" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>4592</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3/\ln(r_{3}^{-1})&lt;4592</annotation></semantics></math>.
By (<a href="#S4.E7" title="In Proof. ‣ 4.2. Assuring desired features of the distinguished sets ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a>),</span></p>
