<p id="S4.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">and note that by Rules <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2^{\circ})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2^{\circ})</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4^{\circ})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4^{\circ})</annotation></semantics></math> and Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma 9. ‣ 3. Tools ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, for every <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
