<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle E(F_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle E(F_{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E31.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle E_{0}\smallsetminus E(F_{1})=(E^{\prime}_{0}\smallsetminus E(H))\cup(T_{0}\smallsetminus T_{U})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle E_{0}\smallsetminus E(F_{1})=(E^{\prime}_{0}\smallsetminus E(H))\cup(T_{0}\smallsetminus T_{U}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Note that by (<a href="#S4.E27" title="In 4.4. A decomposition of 𝐻_0 to two locally irregular subgraphs ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.27</span></a>), (<a href="#S4.E29" title="In 4.4. A decomposition of 𝐻_0 to two locally irregular subgraphs ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.29</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.E30" title="In 4.4. A decomposition of 𝐻_0 to two locally irregular subgraphs ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.30</span></a>),
for every <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V\smallsetminus U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V\smallsetminus U</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S4.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
