<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5. </span>Justification of no degree conflicts in <math id="S4.SS5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider any edge <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="vw\in E_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">vw\in E_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. By (<a href="#S4.E31" title="In 4.4. A decomposition of 𝐻_0 to two locally irregular subgraphs ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.31</span></a>) it must be an edge of either <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We shall exhibit that in each of these cases its ends shall have distinct degrees in the corresponding graph.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose first that <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v,w\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v,w\in U</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="vw\in U^{e}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>e</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">vw\in U^{e}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, by (<a href="#S4.E31" title="In 4.4. A decomposition of 𝐻_0 to two locally irregular subgraphs ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.31</span></a>), <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="vw\in F_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">vw\in F_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S4.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{F_{2}}(v)=d_{H_{0}}(v)-|T_{v}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{F_{2}}(v)=d_{H_{0}}(v)-|T_{v}|</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{F_{2}}(w)=d_{H_{0}}(w)-|T_{w}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{F_{2}}(w)=d_{H_{0}}(w)-|T_{w}|</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, by (<a href="#S4.E25" title="In 4.4. A decomposition of 𝐻_0 to two locally irregular subgraphs ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.25</span></a>), <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{F_{2}}(v)\neq d_{F_{2}}(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{F_{2}}(v)\neq d_{F_{2}}(w)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose next that <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v,w\in V\smallsetminus U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v,w\in V\smallsetminus U</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, by Rule <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1^{\circ})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1^{\circ})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{v}\neq O_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{v}\neq O_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="vw\in E(F_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">vw\in E(F_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, this and (<a href="#S4.E32" title="In 4.4. A decomposition of 𝐻_0 to two locally irregular subgraphs ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.32</span></a>) immediately imply that
<math id="S4.SS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{F_{1}}(v)\neq d_{F_{1}}(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{F_{1}}(v)\neq d_{F_{1}}(w)</annotation></semantics></math> (even modulo <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=2K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=2K</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Otherwise, if <math id="S4.SS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="vw\in E(F_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">vw\in E(F_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, then by (<a href="#S4.E30" title="In 4.4. A decomposition of 𝐻_0 to two locally irregular subgraphs ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.30</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.E31" title="In 4.4. A decomposition of 𝐻_0 to two locally irregular subgraphs ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.31</span></a>), <math id="S4.SS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="vw\notin R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">vw\notin R</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus,
<math id="S4.SS5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|O_{v}-O_{w}|_{K}&gt;\frac{sd+7}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|O_{v}-O_{w}|_{K}&gt;\frac{sd+7}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (as <math id="S4.SS5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{v}\neq O_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{v}\neq O_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>).
Hence, <math id="S4.SS5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|2O_{v}-2O_{w}|_{2K}&gt;sd+7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|2O_{v}-2O_{w}|_{2K}&gt;sd+7</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, by (<a href="#S4.E32" title="In 4.4. A decomposition of 𝐻_0 to two locally irregular subgraphs ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.32</span></a>),</p>
<table id="S4.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.33)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|d_{F_{1}}(v)-d_{F_{2}}(w)|&gt;sd+6." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|d_{F_{1}}(v)-d_{F_{2}}(w)|&gt;sd+6.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS5.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Moreover, by (<a href="#S4.E19" title="In 4.3. Decomposition of 𝐺 to two similar subgraphs ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.19</span></a>),</p>
<table id="S4.E34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.34)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|d_{H_{0}}(v)-d_{H_{0}}(w)|&lt;sd+6." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|d_{H_{0}}(v)-d_{H_{0}}(w)|&lt;sd+6.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS5.p5.3" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S4.SS5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{F_{2}}(v)=d_{H_{0}}(v)-d_{F_{1}}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{F_{2}}(v)=d_{H_{0}}(v)-d_{F_{1}}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{F_{2}}(w)=d_{H_{0}}(w)-d_{F_{1}}(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{F_{2}}(w)=d_{H_{0}}(w)-d_{F_{1}}(w)</annotation></semantics></math>,
then (<a href="#S4.E33" title="In 4.5. Justification of no degree conflicts in 𝐹_1 and 𝐹_2 ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.33</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.E34" title="In 4.5. Justification of no degree conflicts in 𝐹_1 and 𝐹_2 ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.34</span></a>) imply that <math id="S4.SS5.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{F_{2}}(v)\neq d_{F_{2}}(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{F_{2}}(v)\neq d_{F_{2}}(w)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">It thus remains to consider the case when <math id="S4.SS5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V\smallsetminus U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V\smallsetminus U</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in U</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that then, by (<a href="#S4.E30" title="In 4.4. A decomposition of 𝐻_0 to two locally irregular subgraphs ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.30</span></a>), (<a href="#S4.E22" title="In 4.4. A decomposition of 𝐻_0 to two locally irregular subgraphs ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.22</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.E24" title="In 4.4. A decomposition of 𝐻_0 to two locally irregular subgraphs ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.24</span></a>), we have</p>
<table id="S4.E35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.35)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{F_{1}}(w)=|T_{w}|&lt;d_{1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{F_{1}}(w)=|T_{w}|&lt;d_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose first that <math id="S4.SS5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="vw\in F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">vw\in F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that by (<a href="#S4.E30" title="In 4.4. A decomposition of 𝐻_0 to two locally irregular subgraphs ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.30</span></a>), (<a href="#S4.E28" title="In 4.4. A decomposition of 𝐻_0 to two locally irregular subgraphs ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.28</span></a>), (<a href="#S4.E11" title="In 4.3. Decomposition of 𝐺 to two similar subgraphs ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.11</span></a>), (<a href="#S4.E14" title="In 4.3. Decomposition of 𝐺 to two similar subgraphs ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.14</span></a>), Claim <a href="#Thmtheorem10" title="Claim 10. ‣ 4.2. Assuring desired features of the distinguished sets ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> and (<a href="#S4.E21" title="In 4.4. A decomposition of 𝐻_0 to two locally irregular subgraphs ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.21</span></a>),</p>
<table id="S4.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E36"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
