</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Assume finally that <math id="S4.SS5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="vw\in F_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">vw\in F_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, by (<a href="#S4.E31" title="In 4.4. A decomposition of 𝐻_0 to two locally irregular subgraphs ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.31</span></a>), (<a href="#S4.E35" title="In 4.5. Justification of no degree conflicts in 𝐹_1 and 𝐹_2 ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.35</span></a>), (<a href="#S4.E15" title="In 4.3. Decomposition of 𝐺 to two similar subgraphs ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.15</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.E21" title="In 4.4. A decomposition of 𝐻_0 to two locally irregular subgraphs ‣ 4. Proof of Theorem ‣ Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.21</span></a>),</p>
